  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000106",
  "title": "Langton's Ant",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Langton's Ant\n\n**Langton's Ant** is a two-dimensional Turing machine with remarkably simple rules that produce surprisingly complex behavior. Invented by Chris Langton in 1986, it operates on a grid of black and white cells.\n\n## Rules\n\nThe ant follows just two rules based on the color of its current cell:\n\n1. **On a white cell**: Turn **right** 90°, flip the cell to **black**, move forward.\n2. **On a black cell**: Turn **left** 90°, flip the cell to **white**, move forward.\n\n## Emergent Complexity\n\nStarting from a blank grid, the ant's path appears **chaotic** for the first ~10,000 steps, creating a seemingly random blob. Then, suddenly, it begins building a diagonal **\"highway\"** — an infinitely repeating pattern of 104 steps that carries the ant away in a straight line.\n\nThis abrupt transition from chaos to order is one of the most striking examples of emergent behavior in simple systems. No one has proven *why* the highway always appears.\n\n## This Notebook\n\nWe simulate 12,000 steps on a 200×200 grid. Black cells appear dark, and the ant's current position is highlighted in red.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Simulation & Render",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\nconst SIZE: usize = 200;\nconst STEPS: usize = 12_000;\nconst PX: u32 = 3;\n\n// Grid: false = white, true = black\nlet mut grid = vec![vec![false; SIZE]; SIZE];\n\n// Ant: position and direction (0=up, 1=right, 2=down, 3=left)\nlet (mut ax, mut ay, mut dir) = (SIZE / 2, SIZE / 2, 0u8);\n\nfor _ in 0..STEPS {\n    if grid[ay][ax] {\n        dir = (dir + 3) % 4; // On black: turn left\n    } else {\n        dir = (dir + 1) % 4; // On white: turn right\n    }\n    grid[ay][ax] = !grid[ay][ax]; // Flip cell color\n\n    // Move forward (toroidal wrapping)\n    match dir {\n        0 => ay = if ay == 0 { SIZE - 1 } else { ay - 1 },\n        1 => ax = if ax + 1 >= SIZE { 0 } else { ax + 1 },\n        2 => ay = if ay + 1 >= SIZE { 0 } else { ay + 1 },\n        3 => ax = if ax == 0 { SIZE - 1 } else { ax - 1 },\n        _ => unreachable!(),\n    }\n}\n\n// Render the grid as SVG\nlet (img_w, img_h) = (SIZE as u32 * PX, SIZE as u32 * PX);\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (img_w, img_h))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(200, 200, 200)).unwrap();\n\n    let black = RGBColor(30, 30, 40);\n    let ant_color = RGBColor(220, 50, 50);\n\n    // Draw black cells (merging horizontal runs for compact SVG)\n    for y in 0..SIZE {\n        let mut x = 0;\n        while x < SIZE {\n            if grid[y][x] {\n                let start = x;\n                while x < SIZE && grid[y][x] { x += 1; }\n                root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n                    [((start as u32 * PX) as i32, (y as u32 * PX) as i32),\n                     ((x as u32 * PX) as i32 - 1, ((y as u32 + 1) * PX) as i32 - 1)],\n                    black.filled(),\n                )).unwrap();\n            } else {\n                x += 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Highlight ant position in red\n    root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n        [((ax as u32 * PX) as i32, (ay as u32 * PX) as i32),\n         (((ax as u32 + 1) * PX) as i32 - 1, ((ay as u32 + 1) * PX) as i32 - 1)],\n        ant_color.filled(),\n    )).unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_1\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "The Highway Phenomenon",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The Highway Phenomenon\n\nAfter approximately 10,000 steps of seemingly chaotic wandering, the ant discovers a periodic diagonal highway and begins extending it indefinitely. This highway:\n\n- Has a **period of 104 steps** — after 104 moves, the pattern repeats, shifted diagonally.\n- Emerges **regardless of starting conditions** (as far as anyone has tested), though the onset time varies.\n- Remains **unproven** — no mathematical proof explains why the transition always occurs.\n\nThe messy region in the center is the initial chaotic phase. The diagonal stripe extending from it is the highway.\n\n### Try Modifying\n\n- Increase the step count to 50,000+ to see a longer highway.\n- Try starting on a non-blank grid (random initial cells) — the highway still emerges!\n- Implement **multi-color extensions** (e.g., \"LLRR\" or \"LRRRRRLLR\" rule strings) for dramatically different behavior.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ]
}
